# Academic writing style

Before drafting, rewriting, or polishing any prose in a paper (abstract, introduction,
preliminaries, method, results, captions, conclusions), read
`.cursor/skills/academic-writing-style/SKILL.md` and follow it.

That skill encodes our group's voice and structural conventions, distilled only from 13 of the
group's own published papers. Do not substitute generic academic-writing habits for it.

Quick reminders, so small edits do not need the full skill:

- Wrap every drafted passage in `\generatedstyle` (`HTML 1B5E20` and bold). Never the default `green`.
  Beside the colour, mark the model and settings with `\genopen`/`\genclose` (e.g. `grok4.6high`).
  Do not switch to a faster model; quality is not to be traded for speed.
- Raise every open question twice: in chat **and** as a self-contained `\todo{}` at the exact spot in
  the document. Remove the `\todo{}` in the same edit that resolves it.
- No first-person singular. No questions, exclamations, or contractions.
- A display equation is part of a sentence and ends in a comma (if a `where` clause follows) or a period.
- No section or subsection ends on a display equation.
- Cite equations as `(8)`, never `Eq. (8)`. Use `Fig. N` and `Table N`.
- No timing or memory number without the hardware named.
- Self-citations in the third person; `to the best of the author's knowledge`, never `our`.

Symbol definitions and the `where` clause are owned by `latex-paper-writing.mdc`.
